00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.180
So that's pretty nice.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.970
Also since Cloud is about
moving past traditional IT

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.340
and unlocking new
paradigms of work,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.010
we know many businesses are
facing a severe shortage

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.210
of skilled labor.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.250
And certifications are
a reasonable method

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.060
organizations use to validate
baseline capabilities

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.130
with a new way of working.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.560
Being Google Cloud
Certified means

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.630
you've demonstrated
the necessary skills

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.450
to leverage Google
technology in a way that

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.570
can transform businesses.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:17.660
Quoting from IT Skills surveys,
overall, decision makers

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.380
reported that certified team
members troubleshoot issues

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.680
and complete projects more
quickly and have fewer skills

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:25.960
gaps.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.400
And here's where we
can introduce training.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.480
We know training is separate
but supportive of certification.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.340
And training is
absolutely associated

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.240
with job satisfaction.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.620
So this second link
cloud.google.com/training is

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.530
where we describe all the
different offers that Google

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.100
has for training along
multiple learning paths.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.260
The learning paths
are each mapped

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.160
to a different certification.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.450
So the learning paths include
cloud infrastructure, data

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.000
and machine learning,
application development,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.420
and so on.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.070
We also offer for training
different learning environments

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.670
to customize the training for
whatever you like or need.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.470
We have on demand
courses where you

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.860
can learn online at your own
pace wherever you are or you

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.700
can get matched with
Google Cloud instructors

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.620
and classroom instruction,
which frequently works better

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.333
for larger groups.

00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:19.250
But perhaps the most
valuable kind of training

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.860
experience that we have
is hands on training.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.370
And for this we have
Qwiklabs hands on quest labs.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.760
And Qwiklabs are easy to
work through guided tours

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.170
of the capabilities needed to
be successful in these job tasks

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:34.620
and job roles.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.680
So this week here
in the dev zone,

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.260
we have a couple of
different lab experiences

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.440
that we'll talk
about a little later.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.720
I want to dive just
a little bit deeper

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.430
into a question we
get asked frequently,

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.960
which is what is the difference
between the associate level

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.730
certifications and
the professional level

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.190
certifications.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.450
And one way we
distinguish between them

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.580
is the associate
cloud engineer is

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.510
a job task based certification.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.830
And the professional
level certifications

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.610
are job role based, job
task versus job role.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.830
A job task based
certification is focused

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.110
on technologies and tools.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:15.020
That's a good spot
for people who

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.700
are trying to find an entry
point into cloud or even

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.140
into GCP.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.510
And it's also a great
pathway to our professional

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.580
certifications.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.940
In this case, we can see that
for associate cloud engineer,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:32.360
we use words like build,
and deploy, and manage,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.780
because the job tasks for
that associate cloud engineer

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.700
are usually centered
around translating

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:41.540
technical requirements
into technical details

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.120
and implementations.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.130
And that's different than our
professional certification,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.900
which adds a couple
of different verbs

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.700
like design, plan, and optimize.

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.620
And it usually takes many
years to acquire the experience

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.010
necessary with the ambiguities
of real world problems

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:01.010
and hands on work to develop
the abilities we look

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.160
for translating
business requirements

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.307
to the technical requirements
needed by a professional.

00:06:08.307 --> 00:06:09.890
That's different
than what we consider

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:11.840
in the associate level.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.380
Now specifically, to
dive a little deeper,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.400
there are proven
strategies to guide you

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.470
whether you're pursuing
associate or professional

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:19.115
levels.

00:06:19.115 --> 00:06:21.213
So Dan is going to
get into that here.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:23.185
DAN SULLIVAN: Thank you.

00:06:23.185 --> 00:06:24.171
Thanks, Mike.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.570
If you've decided you
wanted to become certified,

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.700
probably the biggest
hurdle you're going to face

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.620
is actually passing
the certification exam.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.050
So I'd like to
spend a few minutes

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.480
and just share some
tips and advice for how

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.660
to prepare for the exam,
how to take the exam,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.170
and what you should focus on in
terms of domains of knowledge

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.990
and topic areas.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.460
Now I'm going to talk
specifically about the cloud

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.820
engineering exam.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.610
But at least half of
what I'm going to say

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.870
applies to any of the exams.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.400
And a lot of what I say for
the cloud engineer exam also

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.630
applies for the
architect exam as well.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.235
So first of all, I want to
talk about a study strategy.

00:07:09.235 --> 00:07:11.360
So if you've just decided
you want to get certified

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.693
and you're wondering what's
the first thing I should do,

00:07:13.693 --> 00:07:16.400
I would say go to the
certification page

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.390
for the certification you're
going to take and find

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.310
the exam guide.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:23.840
Google has done
a really good job

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.750
of developing a one page
summary of what's covered

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.250
on these certification exams.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.910
Now in the case of the
cloud engineering exam,

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.130
there are five high level areas.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.850
And it's things like
planning, deploying, securing,

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:40.960
the [? burbs ?] that
Mike was talking about.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.280
And for each of
those domain areas,

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.790
there are very specific tasks
that you as a cloud engineer

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.650
should know how to do.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.070
And they get very specific.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.830
For example, how do you expand
the CIDR block on a subnet

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:54.373
so you have more IP addresses?

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:55.790
It's that level
of detail that you

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:59.280
need to know when
you take this test.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.690
So if you're not sure,
and even if you are sure

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.860
you're in good shape,
I would suggest

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.720
taking the practice exam.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:08.210
Google has a 20
question practice

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:12.050
exam which is available
from the certification page.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.150
And they give
detailed explanations

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:16.680
of why an answer is
correct or incorrect.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.175
So it's really helpful to
identify your weak areas.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.165
Oh, and one last thing,
if I say nothing else,

00:08:27.165 --> 00:08:28.540
probably the most
important thing

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.660
is that whatever the
tasks are and however

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.240
familiar you are
with certain things,

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.039
it's important that you actually
have hands on experience.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.100
So I would say get
into the Cloud Shell,

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:42.770
get under the Cloud Console,
and do these tasks manually.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.800
This is not the kind
of an exam that you

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.920
can pass by just reading a
book or reading documentation.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.508
Now if you haven't
been in school awhile,

00:08:53.508 --> 00:08:55.050
you haven't taken
an exam in a while,

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.510
you might wonder what
it's going to be like.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.600
Well, the exams are all online.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.130
They are multiple choice.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.200
15 questions.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.020
You have two hours
to take the exam.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.960
And one of the nice things
is that you can go back.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.780
You're not locked out
once you submit an answer.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.710
So you can mark
questions for review.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.970
You can go back,
read things over.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.220
And I find that's really
helpful when I take the exam.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.500
I kind of blow through the
ones I know right away.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.750
And the ones I'm going to
have to read and think about

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:26.460
a little bit I go back for.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.440
And then the key thing
about taking the exam

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.290
is it's really important to
read the question carefully.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:35.580
Now I assume that every word in
that question means something.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.340
And what I look for in addition
to just understanding what's

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.590
the question asking,
I'm also looking

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:44.520
for are there names of
any services or software

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.410
that I should be
paying attention to.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:48.970
And what are the
technical requirements?

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.830
So for example, if I see a word
like or a software like Apache

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.260
Spark and then I
also see a reference

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.130
to customer requirement
wanting a managed service,

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.880
I'm immediately going
to think of Dataproc

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.450
and I'm going to start looking
for answers that have those.

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:07.740
One of the things I've found
taking these various tests

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:11.850
is when it's a four choice
multiple or four choice option,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.940
I can usually narrow it down
pretty quickly to two options.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.960
So one of those is correct and
one of them is a near miss.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.200
Now what I do at that point
is I will look and figure out

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.573
exactly what the difference
is between the two options

00:10:25.573 --> 00:10:27.990
and then go back to the text
of the question to figure out

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.730
what's the distinguishing
characteristic

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:31.397
in the requirements
that's going to help

00:10:31.397 --> 00:10:33.730
me decide between those two.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:36.907
So for example, if I've narrowed
down the options to a question,

00:10:36.907 --> 00:10:38.490
say picking a database,
and it's going

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.575
to be either Data Store or Cloud
SQL, I'll go back to the text.

00:10:42.575 --> 00:10:44.700
And I'll look for a
requirement that says something

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.020
about maybe a flexible
schema in which case

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:52.440
I'll look at Data Store, or if
I require SQL, then of course

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.000
I'll look for Cloud SQL.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.270
Now in terms of what
you need to know,

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:01.258
I think there's two levels here.

00:11:01.258 --> 00:11:03.800
There's stuff that you really
need to know in depth and stuff

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.890
you need to be familiar with, be
able to understand at a higher

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:08.610
level.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.800
So we'll cover both of those.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.700
So with the cloud, as you
probably heard over and over

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.450
again, it's security first.

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.850
So when you go into that
cloud engineer exam,

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.810
it's really important that you
understand identity and access

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.450
management or IAM
and networking.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.670
You really just have
to have that down cold.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:29.750
And for IAM, the key thing
to understand is that--

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.900
excuse me.

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.800
The key thing to understand is
the roles and the permissions

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.770
and how those are structured.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.860
Now prior to IAM,
Google had something

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.020
called primitive roles.

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.890
So if you've seen
things like owner,

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:45.920
editor, viewer, these
very coarse grained roles,

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.620
those existed prior to IAM.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:50.600
So did something
called access scopes.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:51.710
They're still around.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.980
Those are considered--
in the case of the roles

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.750
are considered primitive roles.

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.780
And in general it's best
practice not to use those.

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.350
Best practice is to use IAM and
use fine grained permissions

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:06.890
and roles when you're
implementing your authorization

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:09.170
controls.

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:12.530
On the networking side of the
world, the key things to know

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:13.820
are virtual private clouds.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:16.430
You just need to understand
what those are and the component

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:20.250
structures within them like
subnets and CIDR blocks,

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:22.700
which are used to
specify IP address

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:25.060
ranges within the subnets.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:27.620
Fire rules are
important to know.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.223
Also understand
different options

00:12:29.223 --> 00:12:30.890
when you're working
with a hybrid cloud.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.057
How are you going to connect
your on premise network

00:12:33.057 --> 00:12:34.040
to the Google Cloud?

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.540
And so you want to
understand things

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:37.325
like direct peering and VPN.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.010
Also in terms of
things you really

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.860
need to know in depth,
definitely cloud and storage

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.520
options--

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.260
excuse me-- compute
and storage options.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.070
So with Compute Engine,
you almost certainly

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:55.497
want to know when to use
preemptable machines.

00:12:55.497 --> 00:12:57.830
You want to understand what
is an instance group, what's

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.632
an instance template,
and why do you use them.

00:13:00.632 --> 00:13:02.840
You want to make sure you
understand the role of load

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:05.278
balancers and auto scalers.

00:13:05.278 --> 00:13:07.070
With the App Engine,
you want to understand

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:09.170
that's a platform as a
service and that there

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.463
are two types or two
versions of App Engine now.

00:13:12.463 --> 00:13:13.880
There's one called
standard, which

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.630
is the one that's been
around the longest.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:18.170
And that's basically a
language specific sandbox where

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.190
you can run your application.

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.670
And then there's also
App Engine Flexible,

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.935
which allows you to
bring your own container.

00:13:24.935 --> 00:13:26.810
So you have much more
control over setting up

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.090
your runtime environment.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:30.740
Kubernetes is really important.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:32.490
It's getting even
more important.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.840
So when you take the
cloud engineering exam,

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:37.100
makes sure you
understand both the G

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.100
Cloud and the Kube
control commands that are

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:44.180
used to manage Kubernetes.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:48.560
Also understand the architecture
at a high level of Kubernetes

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:50.300
and how it works, and
understand the idea

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:53.450
that services are implemented
through deployments.

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:57.770
And deployments are implemented
within pods which run on nodes.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.590
It's important to know
that kind of thing.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.680
With regards to cloud
functions, those are very short

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.390
running pieces of code.

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:08.430
And they're really designed
for event processing.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.120
So if you have a question about
a customer uploading an image

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.210
to cloud storage or someone
dropping a message on a Pub/Sub

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:17.770
queue, and you need to do
something in response to that,

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:21.960
cloud functions might be
the right option for that.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:25.520
Now on the storage side,
it's important to understand

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:29.490
cloud storage in detail.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.730
A topic that comes up
over and over again

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.240
when I'm reading various
things about cloud storage

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.070
is the different
kinds of cloud storage

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.520
with regards to multi-regional,
regional, nearline,

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:41.355
and coldline.

00:14:41.355 --> 00:14:42.980
So you want to make
sure you understand

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.355
what all four of
those categories

00:14:44.355 --> 00:14:46.890
are, when you would use
one versus the other.

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.920
And also you want to
understand the lifecycle.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:51.590
So for example, you
can go from regional

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.080
to nearline to coldline, but
you can't go from coldline

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.060
back to regional.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:57.897
Those are the kinds
of things that if you

00:14:57.897 --> 00:14:59.480
have that kind of
information in mind,

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.610
you understand the
restrictions on various things,

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:04.400
it can help you answer
questions more quickly

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.480
because you can eliminate
some of the answer options.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:11.490
Cloud Pub/Sub is a
messaging system.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.820
So it's important to understand
just messaging structure,

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:18.788
why it's used in microservices
and things like that.

00:15:18.788 --> 00:15:21.080
It's not a complicated topic,
but it's definitely worth

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.730
studying.

00:15:22.730 --> 00:15:25.370
Stackdriver like
Kubernetes is becoming

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:26.930
more and more important.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.750
And Stackdriver actually
has three main components.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:30.800
It's a logging service.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:34.220
It has a trace service
and also a debugger.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.283
So you want to understand
each of those three

00:15:36.283 --> 00:15:37.700
and how they're
different and when

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.860
you would use each of them.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.360
Now those were the
things you really

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.340
need to understand as a
cloud engineer in depth.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.415
You also need to
understand at a high level

00:15:50.415 --> 00:15:53.880
all of the services that are
available in Google Cloud.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.490
So while you wouldn't
expect a cloud engineer

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.160
to know how to,
say, optimize a data

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:03.690
model in a relational database
or design a document data

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:07.380
store, but you do need
to understand when

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.610
you would use each of those.

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.910
So for example, if
you hear that you

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.880
need a relational database
or SQL is required,

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:18.300
you need to know that your SQL
options are Cloud SQL, Cloud

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.280
Spanner, and BigQuery.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.140
And then you eliminate--

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.812
then you can narrow it down from
there in terms of your options.

00:16:25.812 --> 00:16:27.270
If something
requires-- if you need

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.080
relational and
global transactions,

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.080
well, then you're going
to use Cloud Spanner.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:33.413
If you're looking to build a
data warehouse or some kind

00:16:33.413 --> 00:16:36.960
of analytic application and
you want SQL for data analysts

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.790
who like to do ad hoc
querying, then BigQuery's

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:44.420
the appropriate option there.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.600
So just understand
for example Bigtable

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:49.600
If you're talking about
IoT or any application

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:53.680
where you need streaming
high volume ingestion

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.050
low latency rights, that's
a great option for Bigtable.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:02.290
Now you also want to understand
some of the services that

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:06.589
are used for moving data around
like Dataflow and Dataprep

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:11.200
and then also [INAUDIBLE]
some of the data

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:13.480
and analyzing data
like Dataproc.

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:17.569
So Cloud Dataproc is the
managed Hadoop Spark Service.

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:22.920
Cloud Dataflow is Google Cloud's
implementation of Apache.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:28.119
It's a unified platform for both
batch and stream processing.

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:29.980
Dataprep's more like
an ETL tool which

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:34.960
lets you work with your data
and prep that for ingestion.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.065
Datalab is used for data
analysis, data exploration,

00:17:39.065 --> 00:17:41.440
and then there are several
others like Deployment Manager

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:43.480
and developer services,
which again you

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.250
don't need to know in
depth, but you just

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:49.150
need to understand what they
provide, and where they fit,

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.225
and how to choose between them.

00:17:52.225 --> 00:17:54.100
With that, I'm going to
turn it back to Mike.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:55.080
There we go.

